All tests green, only the pre-existing goconst warning remains. Both fixes are in place.

---

## Explanation of the stale `origin/HEAD` finding (not fixed)

Codex is right that this is still a hole; I'll explain the mechanism and the fix shape so you can decide when to close it.

**The hole in `defaultBranchRef` (`mode_local.go:259-272`):**

```go
func defaultBranchRef(ctx context.Context, repoRoot string) string {
    if out, ok := runGitOutput(ctx, repoRoot, "symbolic-ref", "--quiet", "refs/remotes/origin/HEAD"); ok {
        ref := strings.TrimSpace(out)
        if strings.HasPrefix(ref, "refs/remotes/") {
            return strings.TrimPrefix(ref, "refs/remotes/")   // ← returns unchecked
        }
    }
    for _, candidate := range []string{"origin/main", "origin/master", "main", "master"} {
        if _, ok := runGitOutput(ctx, repoRoot, "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", candidate); !ok {
            continue
        }
        if _, ok := runGitOutput(ctx, repoRoot, "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", candidate, "HEAD"); ok {
            return candidate   // ← ancestor check applied here
        }
    }
    return ""
}
```

The `merge-base --is-ancestor` ancestor check only runs in the *fallback* loop. The `origin/HEAD` fast-path returns whatever symbolic-ref says without verifying the target is an ancestor of HEAD. Two realistic ways this goes wrong:

1. **Default-branch rename.** Repo renamed `master` → `main`. `git clone` from years ago set `origin/HEAD -> refs/remotes/origin/master`. No-one ran `git remote set-head origin -a` since. The ref still resolves; `master` now points wherever it last was (possibly stale or diverged). `branchLocalRevRange` returns `origin/master..HEAD`, and "feature work" in a dispatch includes every commit on the new `main` that was made since the rename.

2. **Remote config drift.** Someone manually set `git remote set-head origin <old-branch>` for local convenience, or a tool did. `origin/HEAD` now points at a ref that isn't the real default branch. Same leak.

In both cases, `git log origin/master..HEAD` silently produces the wrong set, and the symptom the fix was supposed to prevent (pre-branch history leaking into a feature-branch dispatch) comes back — but harder to notice because `origin/HEAD` *feels* authoritative.

**The fix shape when you're ready:**

Apply the same `merge-base --is-ancestor` guard to the `origin/HEAD` path:

```go
if out, ok := runGitOutput(ctx, repoRoot, "symbolic-ref", "--quiet", "refs/remotes/origin/HEAD"); ok {
    ref := strings.TrimSpace(out)
    if strings.HasPrefix(ref, "refs/remotes/") {
        candidate := strings.TrimPrefix(ref, "refs/remotes/")
        if _, ok := runGitOutput(ctx, repoRoot, "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", candidate, "HEAD"); ok {
            return candidate
        }
    }
}
// fallback loop unchanged
```

Plus a regression test: init a repo, set `origin/HEAD` to a ref on a disjoint history (or force-update it), and assert `defaultBranchRef` returns `""`. The existing `TestDefaultBranchRef_RejectsStaleRefNotAncestorOfHEAD` is the template — it does exactly this for the fallback path, just needs a sibling that exercises the symbolic-ref branch.

Want me to commit the HTTPS-guard fix + `-h` visibility now? Happy to do the stale-`origin/HEAD` fix in a follow-up commit if you'd like, or fold it in.

